SUMMARY:[現場]  The Long History of Amanuenses: Aides to Scholars and Aut
 hors from Antiquity to AI
LOCATION:East Asian Library\, 338
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-long-history-of-amanuenses-aides-
 to-scholars-and-authors-from-antiquity-to-ai-6807
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-long-history-of-amanuense
 s-aides-to-scholars-and-authors-from-antiquity-to-ai-6807\n\nThe technique
 s for making texts have changed across multiple media and historical conte
 xts since antiquity\, but a regular feature of the work of authors and sch
 olars through all those changes is the reliance on helpers or amanuenses. 
 Blair will discuss the factors that have made them both invisible and occa
 sionally visible\, the kind of work they did\, and offer some examples of 
 their trajectories in a few different periods. We can ponder together the 
 extent to which AI fits into and deviates from earlier patterns of assista
 nce. \n\nAnn Blair is Carl H. Pforzheimer University Professor in the Depa
 rtment of History at Harvard University where she teaches book history and
  early modern European cultural and intellectual history. Her research foc
 uses on the working methods of scholars and authors ca. 1500-1700.  She ha
 s studied for example: practices of reading and note-taking\, of composing
  and using reference works and finding devices (in Too Much to Know: manag
 ing scholarly information before the modern age\, 2010)\, paratexts (in th
 e Panizzi lectures of 2019 and L’Entour du texte: la publication du livr
 e savant à la Renaissance\, 2021)\, and the role of amanuenses (In the Sc
 holar’s Workshop: hidden histories of collaboration and authorship\, 202
 6). She has also co-edited various volumes\, most recently: New Horizons f
 or Early Modern Scholarship (2021) with Nicholas Popper\, and Information:
  a historical companion (2021) with Paul Duguid\, Anja Goeing\, and Anthon
 y Grafton.
